Given each symbol represents a distinct digit, determine the value of $w+x+y+z$.

[asy]
label("$w$",(1,0),E);
label("$x$",(2,0),E);
label("$y$",(3,0),E);
label("$z$",(1,-1),E);
label("$y$",(2,-1),E);
label("$w$",(3,-1),E);
label("+",(-2,-1),E);
draw((-2.1,-1.4)--(4.1,-1.4),linewidth(0.5));
label("1",(0,-2),E);
label("1",(1,-2),E);
for (int i =1; i<3; ++i) {
label("0",(1+i,-2),E);
}
[/asy]

Given the function $f(x)=\ln x-ax^{2}+x$.  
$(1)$ If $f(x) \lt 0$ holds for any real number $x \in (0,+\infty)$, find the range of the real number $a$;  
$(2)$ When $a=\frac{1}{2}$, if $f(x_{1})+f(x_{2})=1$, find the minimum value of $x_{1}+x_{2}$.

Given the complex number $z=a^2-7a+6+(a^2-5a-6)i$ ($a\in\mathbb{R}$), find the values of the real number $a$ for which $z$ is:  
(1) a real number;  
(2) an imaginary number;  
(3) a pure imaginary number.

There are  $ 25$  towns in a country. Find the smallest  $ k$  for which one can set up two-direction flight routes connecting these towns so that the following conditions are satisfied:
1) from each town there are exactly  $ k$  direct routes to  $ k$  other towns;
2) if two towns are not connected by a direct route, then there is a town which has direct routes to these two towns.

In the diagram, $ABCD$ is a trapezoid with an area of $24$ square units. $CD$ is three times the length of $AB$. What is the area of $\triangle ABC$?

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(1,4)--(7,4)--(10,0)--cycle);
draw((7,4)--(0,0));
label("$D$",(0,0),W);
label("$A$",(1,4),NW);
label("$B$",(7,4),NE);
label("$C$",(10,0),E);
[/asy]

For how many positive integral values of $a$ is it true that $x = 3$ is the only positive integer solution of the system of inequalities $$
\begin{cases}
4x > 5x - 5 \\
4x - a > -8
\end{cases}
$$

In the diagram, $PQR$ is a straight line, and point $T$ is below the line, making triangle $QRT$. What is the value of $x$ if $\angle PQS = 124^\circ$ and $\angle QRT = 76^\circ$? Assume $\angle QRS = x$ and $S$ is a point such that $\triangle QRS$ and $\triangle QRT$ share side $QR$.

[asy]
draw((-2,0)--(8,0),linewidth(0.7)); draw((8,0)--(5,-5.5)--(0,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((8,0)--(3,-3.8)--(0,0),linewidth(0.7));  // New triangle QRT
label("$P$",(-2,0),W); label("$Q$",(0,0),N); label("$R$",(8,0),E); label("$S$",(5,-5.5),S); label("$T$",(3,-3.8),S);
label("$124^\circ$",(0,0),SW); label("$x^\circ$",(7,0),S);
label("$76^\circ$",(4,-3.8),S);
[/asy]

The figure shown is a cube. The distance between vertices $A$ and $G$ of the cube is $5\sqrt{3}$ units. What is the volume of the cube, in cubic units?

[asy]
size(3cm,3cm);
pair A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H;

A=(0,0);
D=(1,0);
C=(1.5,0.5);
B=(0.5,0.5);
E=(0,1);
H=(1,1);
G=(1.5,1.5);
F=(0.5,1.5);

draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(E--F--G--H--cycle);
draw(A--E);
draw(B--F);
draw(C--G);
draw(D--H);

label("$A$", A, W);
label("$G$", G, NE);

dot(A);
dot(G);
[/asy]

A rectangle $ABCD$ contains four small squares within it. Three squares are shaded and have non-overlapping interiors, as shown in the diagram. The side length of each smaller shaded square is $2$ inches. Calculate the area of rectangle $ABCD$.

[asy]
size(150);
pair A = (0,0), D = (6,0), C = (6,4), B = (0,4);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(A--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
filldraw(B--(2,4)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle, gray(0.7), black);
filldraw((2,0)--(4,0)--(4,2)--(2,2)--cycle, gray(0.7), black);
filldraw((4,0)--(6,0)--(6,2)--(4,2)--cycle, gray(0.7), black);
label("$A$",A,WSW);
label("$B$",B,WNW);
label("$C$",C,ENE);
label("$D$",D,ESE);
[/asy]

Given that there exists \\(x \in \mathbb{R}\\), such that the inequality \\(\left| x-1 \right|-\left| x-2 \right| \geqslant t\\) holds.

\\((1)\\) Find the set \\(T\\) of real numbers \\(t\\) that satisfy the condition;

\\((2)\\) If \\(m > 1\\) and \\(n > 1\\), for \\(\forall t \in T\\), the inequality \\(\log_{3}m \cdot \log_{3}n \geqslant t\\) always holds, find the minimum value of \\(m^2 + n^2\\).

Eight students participate in a pie-eating contest. The graph shows the number of pies eaten by each participating student. Sarah ate the most pies and Tom ate the fewest. Calculate how many more pies than Tom did Sarah eat and find the average number of pies eaten by all the students.

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(1pt)+fontsize(10pt));
pair[] yaxis = new pair[10];
for( int i = 0 ; i < 10 ; ++i ){

yaxis[i] = (0,i);

draw(yaxis[i]--yaxis[i]+(17,0));
}
draw((0,0)--(0,9));
draw((17,9)--(17,0));

fill((1,0)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,0)--cycle,grey);
fill((3,0)--(3,4)--(4,4)--(4,0)--cycle,grey);
fill((5,0)--(5,8)--(6,8)--(6,0)--cycle,grey);
fill((7,0)--(7,5)--(8,5)--(8,0)--cycle,grey);
fill((9,0)--(9,7)--(10,7)--(10,0)--cycle,grey);
fill((11,0)--(11,6)--(12,6)--(12,0)--cycle,grey);
fill((13,0)--(13,2)--(14,2)--(14,0)--cycle,grey);
fill((15,0)--(15,1)--(16,1)--(16,0)--cycle,grey);
label("0",yaxis[0],W);
label("1",yaxis[1],W);
label("2",yaxis[2],W);
label("3",yaxis[3],W);
label("4",yaxis[4],W);
label("5",yaxis[5],W);
label("6",yaxis[6],W);
label("7",yaxis[7],W);
label("8",yaxis[8],W);
label("9",yaxis[9],W);
label("Students/Participants",(8.5,0),S);
label("Results of a Pie-Eating Contest",(8.5,9),N);
label(rotate(90)*"$\#$ of Pies Eaten",(-1,4.5),W);
[/asy]

Calculate:

1. $40 + \left(\frac{1}{6} - \frac{2}{3} + \frac{3}{4}\right) \times 12$;
2. $\left(-1\right)^{2021} + |-9| \times \frac{2}{3} + \left(-3\right) \div \frac{1}{5}$.

For how many positive integral values of $b$ is it true that $x = 3$ is the only positive integer solution of the system of inequalities $$
\begin{cases}
3x > 4x - 4 \\
2x - b > -3
\end{cases}
$$

Below is a portion of the graph of a function, $y=q(x)$:

[asy]
import graph; size(6cm); real lsf=0.5; pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black; real xmin=-3.5,xmax=3.5,ymin=-4.25,ymax=6.25;

pen cqcqcq=rgb(0.75,0.75,0.75);
/*grid*/ pen gs=linewidth(0.7)+cqcqcq+linetype("2 2"); real gx=1,gy=1;
for(real i=ceil(xmin/gx)*gx;i<=floor(xmax/gx)*gx;i+=gx) draw((i,ymin)--(i,ymax),gs); for(real i=ceil(ymin/gy)*gy;i<=floor(ymax/gy)*gy;i+=gy) draw((xmin,i)--(xmax,i),gs);

Label laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);

xaxis("",xmin,xmax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true); yaxis("",ymin,ymax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true);
real f2(real x){return sgn(3x-3)*abs(3x-3)^(1/2)+3*sgn(3x-3)*abs(3x-3)^(1/4)+abs(3x-3)^(1/6);}
draw(graph(f2,xmin,xmax),linewidth(1));
clip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);
label("$y=q(x)$",(xmax+0.25,f2(xmax)),E);
[/asy]

The value of $q(2)$ is an integer. What is it?

Consider the equation of a line $y = mx + b$. Using the graph below, find $mb$.
[asy]
size(100,0);
add(shift(-5,-5)*grid(10,10));
draw((-5,0)--(5,0),linewidth(2));
draw((0,-5)--(0,5),linewidth(2));
label("",(5,0),E);
label("",(0,5),N);
draw((0,-4) -- (4,4),blue,Arrows);
[/asy]
Each grid square in the diagram is 1 unit by 1 unit. The line goes through the points $(0, -4)$ and $(4, 4)$.

In the triangle shown, what is the positive difference between the greatest and least possible integral values of \(y\)?

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
pair a,b,c;
b = (1,2); c = (3,0);
draw(a--b--c--a);
label("$y$",a--b,dir(135)); label("7",b--c,dir(45)); label("9",c--a,S);
[/asy]

A point is selected randomly from the portion of the number line shown here. What is the probability that the point is closer to 7 than to 3? Express your answer as a fraction.

[asy]unitsize(0.2inch);
draw((0,0)--(10,0));
draw((0,-0.1)--(0,0.1));
draw((1,-0.1)--(1,0.1));
draw((2,-0.1)--(2,0.1));
draw((3,-0.1)--(3,0.1));
draw((4,-0.1)--(4,0.1));
draw((5,-0.1)--(5,0.1));
draw((6,-0.1)--(6,0.1));
draw((7,-0.1)--(7,0.1));
draw((8,-0.1)--(8,0.1));
draw((9,-0.1)--(9,0.1));
draw((10,-0.1)--(10,0.1));
label("0",(0,-0.1),S);
